.TH ETIQUETTE 1 "2026-08-31" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
etiquette \- the estate's issue-label grammar, and whether a repository follows it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B etiquette
.br
.B etiquette
--path
.br
.B etiquette
.I owner/repo
.br
.B etiquette
.I owner/repo
--apply
.br
.B etiquette
--all
.RI [ --apply ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints the label grammar every repository in this ecosystem follows, and
grades one repository against it.
.PP
The grammar has exactly one home,
.BR bin/lib/labels.tsv ,
and is read at the point of use rather than copied into each repository. A
grammar copied into twenty-four repositories is twenty-four grammars:
stamped copies do not travel, they rot, and the drift detector reports OK
throughout.
.PP
Each row declares a label, its colour, its
.B source
and its meaning. The source column decides who may write the label, and it is
the point of the file:
.TP
.B derived:decision
Historical name only, left as-is in
.B bin/lib/labels.tsv
(a different file, out of this verb's scope) \(em nothing derives it anymore.
.B etiquette
used to read
.BR DECISION: / NO-DECISION:
off line 1 of an issue body and reconcile a
.B needs-human
label against it. That grammar was deleted in hf7y/scheduler#318: GitHub's
own
.B assignees
field is the "waiting on a human" signal now, read directly by scheduler's
.BR tempo (1),
and needs no body to be read at all.
.B etiquette
no longer touches issue bodies or comments \(em it only checks that a
repository carries the labels the grammar declares, whichever verb writes
each one.
.TP
.BI written: verb
That verb applies the label at filing time and nothing else should. A person
retyping one of these is drift.
.TP
.B typed
A judgement a person or agent applies. No mechanism derives it, and none
should claim to.
.PP
A label absent from the grammar is a repository's own business: it is left
alone and not reported. The grammar declares a floor, not a whitelist.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --path
Print the file the grammar was read from. Use this to prove which copy a host
obeyed.
.TP
.B --apply
Create any declared label the repository lacks. Without it, nothing is
written.
.TP
.B --all
Grade every repository named in
.BR bin/lib/roster-set.sh ,
one after another, and report the worst verdict any of them produced. This is
the estate-wide caller, and it is what
.B .github/workflows/etiquette.yml
runs daily with
.BR --apply .
.PP
It sweeps the repository set, not the armed set: a project whose
.B schedule/ROSTER
row reads
.B parked
is still swept, and still expected to carry the labels the grammar declares,
whether or not anything ever dispatches to it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The repository carries every label the grammar declares.
.TP
.B 1
Findings: a declared label is missing from the repository.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND \(em the grammar or the label list could not be read. Never 0: an
unreadable label list is not a clean one, and a grammar that failed to load is
not an empty grammar.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR defere (1),
.BR gh-sign (1)
